 "notes": "Manual: expected = every router.<verb>('<path>') in the Admin and Content API route files (ghost/core/server/web/api/endpoints/{admin,content}/routes.js, read with a regex), prefixed with the real mount: BASE_API_PATH = '/ghost/api' (shared/url-utils.ts) via backendApp.lazyUse(BASE_API_PATH, ...) and apiApp.lazyUse('/admin/' | '/content/', ...) in other files. The routes resolve except for that prefix (prefix-other-file, all 256); lazyUse is Ghost's own express wrapper. The precision sample (whole repo) is the same story for the members app (frontendApp.lazyUse('/members', ...)) and the comments router (membersApp.use('/api/comments', ...)). Calls: the portal, search and admin apps use plain fetch with the site URL from config and the path written out — right; the admin-auth frame builds adminUrl = window.location.href.replace(...) + 'api/admin' and calls `${adminUrl}/comments/...` — the literal api/admin is lost (field-in-interpolation, H2-D12). Since H2b's TS cross-file mount graph (2026-10-16): the 256 come out under /ghost/api/{admin,content} — routes.js exports a function returning its router (module.exports = function apiRoutes() {..; return router}), app.js mounts what it returns (apiApp.use(routes())), api/index.js re-exports (module.exports = require('./app')) and lazyUse is read as a mount because shared/express.js defines it as one (app.use(mountPath, .. requireFn() ..)). The run showed the regex had missed two sub-routers of admin/routes.js mounted in the file itself (router.use('/apps', appsRouter), router.use('/members/metafields', metafieldsRouter)): their 13 routes were added. The members, comments, announcement and gift routes take /members and /gift the same way; the 20 sampled routes were judged again: right.",
